.TH "UPTERM" "1" "Oct 2026" "Upterm 0.0.0+dev" "Upterm Manual"

.SH NAME
upterm-proxy - Proxy a terminal session via WebSocket


.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBupterm proxy [flags] URL\fP


.SH DESCRIPTION
Carry ssh's connection to a session over a WebSocket, as its ProxyCommand:
the SSH line of a host on a ws:// or wss:// relay runs it.

.PP
URL is the ws:// or wss:// URL in the ProxyCommand of the host's SSH line.
upterm join is the simpler way to join such a session: it takes the
connect string on the host's Join line, and needs no ssh options.

.PP
Exit status: 1 when it can't connect to the relay, including a usage or
configuration error. Once connected, whichever end comes first decides: 0
when ssh closes the connection, 1 when the relay closes it or a read or write
fails.


.SH OPTIONS
\fB-h\fP, \fB--help\fP[=false]
	help for proxy

.PP
\fB--proxy\fP=""
	HTTP proxy to reach the relay through (e.g. http://proxy.example.com:3128). Without it, HTTPS_PROXY/HTTP_PROXY are used.


.SH OPTIONS INHERITED FROM PARENT COMMANDS
\fB--debug\fP[=false]
	Enable debug logging (log file: /home/user/.local/state/upterm/upterm.log). A non-empty DEBUG turns it on too.


.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
  # A host shares a session over a WebSocket:
  upterm host --server wss://uptermd.upterm.dev

  # A guest joins with the SSH line the host prints, which runs upterm proxy:
  ssh -o ProxyCommand='upterm proxy wss://ID@uptermd.upterm.dev' ID@uptermd.upterm.dev:443

  # The simpler way:
  upterm join wss://ID@uptermd.upterm.dev
.EE


.SH SEE ALSO
\fBupterm(1)\fP


